Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 13:57:58 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

Re: the discussion between Robin Hanson and Perry Metzger over futures markets.

I have a question about the terminology here:

Perry Metzger writes:

> Allow me to repeat my point again, though. If I am buying an index future
> on the S&P 500 and the index goes up $10 per contract, I have made $10 --
> but the person that sold that contract has lost it, because he must pay
> me those $10. On the other hand, if I buy the S&P 500 as a basket, and
> the index goes up $10, the person who sold me the basket has lost potential
> income, but doesn't have to pay me anything -- the game is positive sum.

Why wouldn't the second case _also_ be a zero sum game? Maybe this is
quibbling, but a "paper loss" is nearly as real to me as a "real" loss. 

(Maybe we need finer distinctions to cover paper losses?)

But I agree more with Perry that futures markets are never positive
sum. Perhaps what Robin is hinting at the people freely engange in
futures markets because of differing valuations on some commodity.

Thus, the farmer writes a contract to hedge against a drop in corn
prices. He care more about getting some acceptable price for his corn
than in taking the risks that corn prices will go way up, or way down.
So, his "peace of mind" has some value (and the commodity speculator
may have an entirely different set of values...).

In this sense, the futures market is a positive sum game, is it not?
(Locally, any given transaction is zero sum, but overall the sum is
positive?)

I'll await the opinions of the experts on this one.

-Tim


