Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 16:57:51 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL: Communities, laws, etc.

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   >However, certain sectors of one's life are governed by physical
   >proximity -- physical safety, protection of property from theft,
   >certain quality of life factors (open space, pollution, visual
   >attractiveness), etc.  So the natural community for contracting law on
   >these matters is a compact physical territory.  In sort, a libertarian
   >version of a town, state, or nation.

   I dont think I believe this premise. You are arguing that there are
   natural geographical monopolies for law enforcement, but I have seen
   little evidence to support this.

Well, safe streets seem to intrinsically involve geography -- I can
only make the streets around of my house safe by keeping thugs off of
it(*), which automatically means making the streets around my neighbor's
house safe.  As for visual atrractiveness and other things commonly
controlled by "zoning", that they have a geographic basis is obvious.

(*) There is a lot of evidence that the level of violent crime is
controlled by putting the violent people *elsewhere*, much more than
it is by discovering and punishing the perpetrators of individual
violent acts.

