Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 17:03:19 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: the free market

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   I often hear the "you libertarians go too far" notion, but I've never
   understood it. There are some solid economic arguments for the free
   market structure, and they seem to apply fairly universally. Every
   once in a while, someone claims that they don't apply to X, where X is
   usually something like the textile industry. However, these
   counterclaims usually are without basis -- they aren't actually
   exceptions to the rule.

First off, I would not that "laissez faire" isn't always the same as a
"market" in the economist's sense of the word.

   Anyway, its all fine and dandy to claim "nothing to excess", but thats
   not really a good rule when dealing with natural laws, is it -- and to
   a real extent the market is an inevitable consequence of nature that
   we have no choice but to live with.

That's rather curious -- if the free market is a law of nature, then how
did the Soviets avoid it for 70 years?  And if they *are* an example
of a free market (in some sense), then what is excluded by that term?

   As for your claim that sometimes there are better ways to structure
   things than the market, I'm willing to hear you out -- but only if you
   provide a concrete statement of when those conditions are and what you
   would propose. I suspect that you don't have anything that others
   haven't tried to pass off before, but you never know.

I'll do that as soon as you define "better"!  For instance, many
people consider the measure of the "goodness" of an economic system to
be the degree of *equality* of levels of income of various people.
(Personally, I disagree.)  But I've never met a libertarian who
doesn't at heart believe that the sole measure of the "goodness" of an
economic system to be the absence of government intervention.

Dale

